'''II.''' ''conj.'' A particle used after comparatives, and certain words which express comparison or diversity, such as ''more'', ''better'', ''other'', ''otherwise'', ''rather'', ''else'', etc., and introducing the second member of a comparison. {{smaller|''Than'' has the same case (usually the nominative) after it as it has before it, in accordance with the syntactical rule that "conjunctions connect . . . the same cases of nouns and pronouns": as, he is taller ''than'' I (am); I am richer ''than'' he (is); "thrice fairer ''than'' (I) myself (am)" (''Shak.'', Venus and Adonis, l. 7); they like you better ''than'' (they like) me.}}

{{smaller block|Thenne was ich al so fayn as foul of fair morwenynge,
Gladder ''than'' gleo-man [is] that gold hath to gyfte.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (C), xii. 103.}}}}

{{smaller block|Among them that are born of women there hath not risen a greater ''than'' John the Baptist; notwithstanding he that is least in the kingdom of heaven is greater ''than'' he.
{{right|Mat. xi. 11.}}}}

{{smaller block|I will sooner trust the wind
With feathers, or the troubled sea with pearl,
''Than'' her with any thing.
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Philaster, v. 5.}}}}

{{smaller block|This age, this worse ''then'' iron age,
This sincke of synne.
{{right|''Times' Whistle'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 2.}}}}

{{smaller block|I am better acquainted with the country ''than'' you are.
{{right|''Cotton'', in Walton's Angler, ii. 225.}}}}

{{smaller block|He [King John] had more of Lightning in him ''than'' [he had] of Thunder.
{{right|''Baker'', Chronicles, p. 75.}}}}

{{smaller block|There is no art that hath bin . . . more soyl'd and slubber'd with aphorisming pedantry ''then'' the art of policie.
{{right|''Milton'', Reformation in Eng., ii.}}}}

{{smaller block|He desires to be answerable no farther ''than'' he is guilty.
{{right|''Swift'', Tale of a Tub, Apol.}}}}

{{smaller block|The late events seem to have no other effect ''than'' to harden them in error.
{{right|''Prescott'', Ferd. and Isa., ii. 7.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|No sooner the bells leave off ''than'' the diligence rattles in.
{{right|''Browning'', Up at a Villa.}}}}

{{smaller|A noun-clause introduced by ''that'' sometimes follows ''than'': as, I had rather be a sufferer myself ''than that'' you should be; and the ''that'' is now and then omitted in poetry.}}

{{smaller block|Since I suppose we are made to be no stronger
''Than'' faults may shake our frames.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. for M., ii. 4. 133.}}}}

{{smaller|Sometimes the preceding comparative is left to be inferred from the context; sometimes it is omitted from mere carelessness. A noun or a pronoun after ''than'' has a show of analogy with one governed by a preposition, and is sometimes blunderingly put in the objective case even when properly of subjective value: as, none knew better ''than him''. Even Milton says ''than whom'', and this is more usual: for example, ''than whom'' there is none better.}}

'''thanage''' (thā′nāj), ''n.'' [< ''thane'' + ''-age''.] (''a'') The dignity or rank of a thane; the state of being a thane. (''b'') The district or territory owned or administered by a thane; also, the tenure by which the thane or baron held it.

'''thanatography''' (than-a̤-tog′ra-fi), ''n.'' [< Gr. θάνατος, death, + -γραφία, < γράφειν, write.] A narrative of one's death: distinguished from ''biography'', a narrative of one's life. ''Thackeray'', Catharine, vi. [Rare.]

'''thanatoid''' (than′a̤-toid), ''a.'' [< Gr. *θανατοειδής, contr. θανατώδης, resembling death, < θάνατος, death (θνήσκειν, θανεῖν, √ θαν, die), + εἶδος, form.] 1. Resembling death; apparently dead. ''Dunglison.''—2. Deadly, as a venomous snake.

'''thanatology''' (than-a̤-tol′ọ̄-ji), ''n.'' [< Gr. θάνατος, death, + -λογία, < λέγειν, say: see ''-ology''.] The doctrine of death; a discourse on death.

'''thanatophidia''' (than″a̤-tō-fid′i-ä̤), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < Gr. θάνατος, death, + NL. ''ophidia''.] Venomous

