'''thank''' (thangk), ''n.'' [< ME. ''thank'', ''thonk'', < AS. ''thanc'', ''thonc'', thought, grace, favor, content, thanks (= OS. ''thanc'' = OFries. ''thonk'', ''thank'' = D. ''dank'' = MLG. ''dank'', ''danke'' = OHG. MHG. ''danc'', G. ''dank'' = Icel. ''thökk'' (''thakk''-), for orig. *''thönk'' (*''thank''-), = Sw. ''tack'' = Dan. ''tak'' = Goth. ''thagks'', thought), < *''thincan'' (pret. *''thanc''), etc., think: see ''think''<sup>1</sup>. For the phonetic relation of ''thank'' to ''think'', cf. that of ''song''<sup>1</sup> (Sc. ''sang'') to ''sing''; for the connection of thought, cf. ''min''<sup>3</sup> (G. ''minne'', etc.), thought, remembrance, love.] 1†. Grateful thought; gratitude; good will.

{{smaller block|This encres of hardynesse and myght<br>Com him of love, his ladyes ''thank'' to winne.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, iii. 1777.}}
He seide, "In ''thank'' I shal it take."
{{right|''Rom. of the Rose'', l. 4577.}}}}

2. Expression of gratitude; utterance of a sense of kindness received; acknowledgment by words or signs of a benefit or favor conferred: now used almost exclusively in the plural.

{{smaller block|To some y<sup>t</sup> are good men God sendeth wealth here also, and they giue hem great ''thanke'' for his gift, and he rewardeth them for the ''thanke'' to.
{{right|''Sir T. More'', Cumfort against Tribulation (1573), fol. 35.}}
If ye love them which love you, what ''thank'' have ye?
{{right|Luke vi. 32.}}
O, good men, eate that good which he hath giuen you, and giue him ''thanks''. {{right|''Purchas'', Pilgrimage, p. 257.}}}}

{{smaller block|[The plural ''thanks'' was sometimes used as a singular.
What a ''thanks'' I owe}}

